This directory is hiqlite 0.16.0 (https://github.com/sebadob/hiqlite, Apache-2.0), taken from
crates.io, with these changes, made for rradius:

1. src/client/stream.rs: RECONNECT_SLOW_INTERVAL is 1 second, not 5. After a leader is lost,
   the client's reconnect loop is what finds the new leader; with 5 seconds, the first write
   after a three-node election (about 6 to 7 seconds) waited until about 15 seconds.
2. tests/ and benches/ are removed, and Cargo.toml has no [[test]], [[bench]] or
   [dev-dependencies] entries: they are not built or run here.
3. Cargo.toml.orig, Cargo.lock, .cargo-ok and .cargo_vcs_info.json are not copied.
4. src/network/tcp_socket.rs: on Linux every connection (the listening socket, accepted and
   outgoing streams) gets TCP_USER_TIMEOUT of 10 seconds, so a connection whose peer was
   partitioned is closed after 10 seconds without an acknowledgement instead of retransmitting
   for minutes; a healed follower then reconnects in about 5 to 7 seconds, not 15 to 47. The
   two tests at the end of that file are not built here (see change 2: the library's own tests
   do not compile without the removed dev-dependencies); they were run in a copy of the file in
   a small crate on Linux.
5. src/client/mgmt.rs: the last step of the shutdown, telling the SQL client's stream task to
   end, is waited for at most 1 second. A node without a leader, with a request already waiting
   for that task, ran into the 20 second shutdown limit there and reported an error, after Raft,
   the log writer and the SQL writer had already stopped. The cache layer's send is unchanged
   (rradius does not build the `cache` feature).
6. src/client/create.rs and src/client/stream.rs: a local client looks for the leader in its
   stream task, before the first connection, not before `new_local` returns. `start_node` of a
   node that has joined its cluster returns while there is no leader; before, a former follower
   restarted without a majority never got a client. Reads through the leader wait meanwhile.
   Until the leader is found the client takes this node for it (`is_leader_db` says so too), so
   a write goes to the local Raft, which names the leader it knows of for the client to send it
   to, and fails at once when it knows none. A remote client is unchanged.

Everything else is as published. To move to a newer hiqlite, take its source the same way and
make changes 1, 4, 5 and 6 again, unless they are no longer needed.

The build prints one rustc warning ("unreachable call" in src/error.rs): it is in the published
code, which cargo only hides for registry crates.
